This is step 4, page 4. After reducing the fraction to an irreducible fraction, if the denominator is 1, it is determined to be an integer (e.g., 6/2 = 3), and if the denominator is not 1, it is determined to be a rational number (e.g., 1/2, minus 3/4) rather than an integer. One thing to be especially careful about at this stage is that you should not be fooled into thinking that it is a ‘rational number, not an integer’ just because it is written in the form of a fraction! If it becomes a natural number or a negative integer when reduced, such as minus 8/4 = -2, it is an integer.

