The final step 6, the proof, is complete! Starting from the starting condition of classifying the inclusion relationship between natural numbers, integers made up of 0, and negative integers, and rational numbers expressed as integers with non-zero denominators, we went through the idea of visualizing the Venn diagram of numbers (natural numbers, integers, rational numbers, real numbers) by corresponding equal points on the number line. All integers can be expressed as fractions with a denominator of 1, so they are included in rational numbers, and rational numbers are not integers and integers. It was clearly confirmed that the conclusion of the number system, which is neatly classified into rational numbers (finite and recurring decimals), holds true. The key point to remember here is that you should not be fooled into thinking that it is a ‘rational number, not an integer’ just because it is written in the form of a fraction! If it becomes a natural number or a negative integer when reduced, such as minus 8/4 = -2, it is an integer. Finally, ask a deeper question. It seems like the number line is densely filled with rational numbers alone, but why is an irrational number (root 2, pi) necessary to fill in the gaps between rational numbers? Experiment by changing the values ​​and shapes directly on the math homepage in the link in the description!
